# ZeroServe implementation plan

Updated 2026-09-12 after implementing the native GeoArrow fixes in DuckSpatial
and deckglgeoarrow.

## 1. Current state

### External integration work implemented, awaiting upstream merge

1. [DuckSpatial PR #150](https://github.com/Cidree/duckspatial/pull/150)
   fixes `nanoarrow::as_nanoarrow_array_stream(x, native = TRUE)`. It now casts
   DuckSpatial's WKB geometry column to a native GeoArrow Point, LineString, or
   Polygon-family layout and preserves DuckSpatial's CRS metadata. The PR is
   open against `dev` and currently mergeable.
2. [deckglgeoarrow PR #19](https://github.com/r-spatial/deckglgeoarrow/pull/19)
   recognizes `duckspatial_df` and delegates conversion to DuckSpatial with
   `native = TRUE`. The PR is open against `main` and currently mergeable.

Together these PRs already provide the direct mapping route:

```text
lazy duckspatial_df
  -> deckglgeoarrow requests native = TRUE
  -> DuckSpatial executes the query and returns native GeoArrow
  -> geoarrowWidget attaches the Arrow data
  -> Deck.gl renders it in a MapGL map
```

ZeroServe is not needed for that direct route and must not duplicate its class
conversion or mapping logic.

### ZeroServe server hardening implemented locally

Commit `926a69b` on `feat/ipc-server-hardening` implements the practical server
hardening work: robust single-range parsing, bounded chunks, `OPTIONS` and
`HEAD`, CORS headers, `Content-Length`, graceful IPC status checks, and temporary
file cleanup. This commit is local work pending integration into `main`.

One architectural cleanup remains: the data request handler still exists both
inside the `callr` process closure and as `.handle_data_request()` for tests.
The two copies should eventually be generated from one source or covered by a
parity test. This is separate from DuckSpatial integration.

One security hardening item also remains from the completed server plan:
`start_server()` still creates its control-plane token with
`rlang::hash(runif(1))`, which depends on R's predictable global RNG. Replace it
with a token containing at least 256 bits from an operating-system-backed
cryptographically secure random source. Keep the token transient and confined
to the parent and background server processes. This is separate from
DuckSpatial integration and should be implemented in a focused server change.

## 2. Architectural boundary

ZeroServe owns transport only:

- serialize an Arrow IPC stream;
- keep the serialized buffer alive through `mori`;
- expose it at a localhost HTTP URL with browser-compatible CORS;
- serve disk files with HTTP Range support.

Other packages retain their existing responsibilities:

- DuckSpatial owns lazy query execution, geometry type detection, WKB-to-native
  GeoArrow conversion, and CRS metadata;
- deckglgeoarrow owns layer/source integration and requests the representation
  required by Deck.gl;
- geoarrowWidget owns widget data attachment and browser dependencies;
- MapGL owns the MapLibre/Mapbox map.

Therefore ZeroServe should delegate a `duckspatial_df` to DuckSpatial's public
nanoarrow S3 method. It should not inspect DuckSpatial SQL, guess geometry
columns, collect through `DBI::dbGetQuery()`, convert through `sf`, or contain
Deck.gl styling code.

## 3. Remaining DuckSpatial integration

Implement this only after DuckSpatial PR #150 is merged, or test against its
feature branch until then.

### Phase A — use DuckSpatial's native stream

Modify `R/utils-arrow.R`:

```r
if (inherits(x, "duckspatial_df")) {
  return(nanoarrow::as_nanoarrow_array_stream(x, native = TRUE))
}
```

Remove the current DuckSpatial-specific `dbplyr::remote_con()`,
`dbplyr::sql_render()`, `DBI::dbGetQuery()`, recursive data-frame conversion,
and the obsolete `bad_weak_ptr` fallback comment. Those steps eagerly collect
the result and bypass DuckSpatial's native GeoArrow implementation.

Modify `R/serve.R` so `.zs_is_duckdb_arrow_input()` does not classify
`duckspatial_df` as the generic DuckDB Arrow route. A `duckspatial_df` must pass
through `as_arrow_stream()` instead. Keep the generic DuckDB connection and
DuckDB-backed `tbl_sql` path unchanged; it serves a different, non-DuckSpatial
contract.

Do not add MapGL, deckglgeoarrow, or geoarrowWidget as dependencies. Keep
DuckSpatial optional in `Suggests`; no explicit `requireNamespace()` is needed
inside this branch because a live `duckspatial_df` already comes from an active
DuckSpatial installation and nanoarrow performs S3 dispatch.

### Phase B — replace the superficial test

The existing test creates a non-spatial table, gives it a `duckspatial_df`
class, and verifies only ordinary columns. It cannot detect the WKB/native
GeoArrow bug.

Replace it with two focused tests:

1. A dispatch test, following deckglgeoarrow PR #19's pattern, registers a
   unique fake subclass method whose default is `native = FALSE`. It verifies
   that ZeroServe requests `native = TRUE` and does not route the object through
   the generic DuckDB/Arrow code.
2. An end-to-end test uses small real spatial data from DuckSpatial, calls
   `zs_serve_arrow()`, downloads the served IPC stream, and inspects the geometry
   child's `ARROW:extension:name`. Assert the exact expected native type, for
   example `geoarrow.point` or `geoarrow.multipolygon`, rather than merely
   checking `^geoarrow\\.`. Also verify ordinary attribute columns survive.

The end-to-end test should skip cleanly when optional packages are unavailable.
It must use bundled, small data so it is deterministic and does not access the
network.

### Phase C — document the URL-based route

Add one concise example to the ZeroServe vignette after the implementation is
green. It should demonstrate the role ZeroServe adds, rather than repeat the
direct object route:

```r
library(duckspatial)
library(zeroserve)
library(mapgl)
library(deckglgeoarrow)

countries <- ddbs_open_dataset(
  system.file("spatial/countries.geojson", package = "duckspatial")
)

url <- zs_serve_arrow(countries, layer_id = "countries")

maplibre(projection = "mercator") |>
  addSource(id = "countries", url = url) |>
  addGeoArrowPolygonLayer(
    source = "countries",
    layer_id = "countries",
    geom_column_name = attr(countries, "sf_column"),
    tooltip = "NAME_ENGL"
  )
```

For web maps, the query must produce longitude/latitude coordinates in the CRS
expected by the renderer. ZeroServe must not silently collect through `sf` just
to reproject; users should apply the transform lazily in DuckSpatial before
serving when needed.

## 4. Memory and performance truth

Use precise terminology in documentation:

- DuckSpatial stays lazy until `zs_serve_arrow()` requests its stream.
- `zs_serve_arrow()` then executes the query and serializes the complete IPC
  stream into an R raw vector before sharing that buffer with the background
  server through `mori`.
- The HTTP server avoids JSON and avoids another R-process copy, but this is not
  end-to-end zero-copy and is not an out-of-core browser pipeline.
- For results too large to fit comfortably in RAM/browser memory, filter or
  aggregate in DuckSpatial first. Use `zs_serve_parquet()`/`zs_serve_file()` for
  disk-backed transport where the consuming frontend supports that format.

Do not advertise ZeroServe as making an arbitrarily large lazy query safe to
render. Query pushdown reduces the input; it does not remove the final
materialization required by the browser.

## 5. Commit and merge order

1. Merge or otherwise integrate ZeroServe commit `926a69b` into ZeroServe
   `main`; keep server hardening separate from the spatial adapter.
2. Merge DuckSpatial PR #150 into DuckSpatial `dev`.
3. Merge deckglgeoarrow PR #19 after its upstream workflow is approved. It
   should continue to depend only on DuckSpatial's public nanoarrow behavior,
   without adding DuckSpatial to `Imports` or `Suggests`.
4. Create a fresh ZeroServe branch from updated `main`, for example
   `feat/duckspatial-native-geoarrow`, containing only Phases A and B.
5. Add the vignette example in the same ZeroServe PR only if the integration
   test passes with the released or installable upstream revisions.

No changes are required in geoarrowWidget or MapGL for this integration.

## 6. Acceptance criteria

- `zs_serve_arrow(duckspatial_df)` calls
  `nanoarrow::as_nanoarrow_array_stream(..., native = TRUE)`.
- The DuckSpatial query is not collected through `DBI::dbGetQuery()` or `sf`.
- A real Point/LineString/Polygon-family test reads the served IPC and observes
  the exact native `geoarrow.*` extension expected for that fixture.
- Attribute columns and CRS extension metadata survive serialization and HTTP
  download.
- Existing `sf`, ordinary data-frame, nanoarrow stream, DuckDB connection, and
  DuckDB-backed lazy-table tests continue to pass.
- The URL returned by ZeroServe can be supplied to
  `deckglgeoarrow::addSource(url = ...)` and rendered in a MapGL map over HTTP
  without `file://` CORS failures.
- ZeroServe gains no mapping dependencies and contains no layer styling logic.

For the separate server-security follow-up:

- IPC control tokens no longer depend on `runif()`, `sample()`, or other R
  global-RNG output.
- Each server start generates a new token with at least 256 bits of entropy
  from an operating-system-backed cryptographically secure random source.
- Existing authenticated control-plane operations and server lifecycle tests
  continue to pass.

## 7. Verification

Run the package's normal checks after Phases A and B:

```bash
Rscript -e 'devtools::test()'
R CMD build .
R CMD check --no-manual zeroserve_*.tar.gz
```

Then manually run the URL-based example above in a fresh R session, open it in
an external browser over HTTP, and verify that the polygons and tooltip render
without console errors. Keep any unrelated transient MapLibre hover issue out
of this PR; if reproducible, it belongs in a separate deckglgeoarrow change.
